Alright, the latest visual audit confirmed that the capsule characters render fine under the “balanced” graphics preset, but there’s that persistent black box artifact when using the “ultra” preset at 1280×720. I reproduced the issue with the dbg‑caps2 script, which set the graphics preset to “ultra” and captured a screenshot (dbg‑ultra.png). The screenshot shows a solid black tile where the scene should be rendered—likely a result of the bloom post‑process smearing a single bad pixel across an entire tile.

Given the rendering pipeline, a common cause for such smear‑type artifacts is a NaN or Inf value leaking into the bloom shader. The bloom blur operates on tiles, and if even one pixel contains NaN/Inf, the convolution can propagate that value, turning the whole tile black (or white, depending on how the shader handles it). In the fragment shader (renderer.js), after applying fog we simply return the computed color. There’s no safeguard against NaNs, and there’s also no clamping of the final color range, which could allow extreme values to overflow.

I recall that earlier I already patched the ray‑intersection code to clamp the denominator (a) to avoid division by near‑zero, which should reduce the chance of NaNs from that path. However, other calculations (e.g., exponential fog factor, lighting, or texture lookups) could still produce NaNs under extreme settings like “ultra”. The safest fix is to sanitize the final color before returning it: check each component for NaN and replace the whole color with black if any component is invalid, then clamp the color to a reasonable upper bound (the engine seems to allow values up to ~80 for HDR). This will prevent the bloom pass from receiving NaNs and should stop the black tile from appearing.

